WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.230
Our school garden: tomato beds sell for

2
00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.790
thirty coins, bean beds for forty.

3
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.240
But land, water and the pupils’

4
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.640
hours are all limited.

5
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.060
How many of each should we plant?

6
00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.470
Today: linear programming

7
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.610
in two dimensions.

8
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.320
Draw the plans, and find the

9
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.380
best one at a corner.

10
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.520
Call the tomato beds x

11
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.860
and the bean beds y.

12
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:31.930
Land: x plus y, at most eight.

13
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.620
Water: two x plus y, at most

14
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.600
fourteen cans a day.

15
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.220
Hours: x plus three y, at

16
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.420
most eighteen a week.

17
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:50.190
Profit: thirty x plus forty y.

18
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.100
On graph paper, each limit

19
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.080
is a straight line.

20
00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.430
For land, the line joins eight beds of

21
00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.590
tomatoes to eight beds of beans.

22
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.780
Plans on the line use all the land.

23
00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.770
Plans below it, towards zero, fit.

24
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.780
Draw all three lines, and shade

25
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.540
where every limit holds.

26
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.120
That shape holds every feasible plan.

27
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.970
Its corners are where lines meet, such

28
00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:22.750
as six and two, or three and five.

29
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.200
Now the goal.

30
00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:30.070
Every plan earning, say, one hundred and

31
00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.770
twenty coins lies on one straight line.

32
00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:38.220
Slide that line up, and the profit grows.

33
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.070
The last point of the shape it

34
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.510
touches is the best plan.

35
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.060
Here it touches the corner three

36
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.720
tomato beds and five bean beds: two

37
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:50.780
hundred and ninety coins.

38
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.520
Straight-line limits and a

39
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.180
straight-line goal make a linear program.

40
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.650
And its best plan is always at a corner.

41
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:01.530
Quick check!

42
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.960
Why is the best plan at a corner, and not

43
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.880
somewhere in the middle of the shape?

44
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.500
From any point inside, the profit

45
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.360
line can still slide further.

46
00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.250
Push it to an edge, then along

47
00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.430
the edge to a corner.

48
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.940
That’s why the simplex method walks

49
00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.780
only from corner to corner.

50
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.350
At the best corner, land and hours are

51
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.190
used up; water has three cans to spare.

52
00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.430
Change what you value, and the

53
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:32.650
best corner can change.

54
00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.990
With tomatoes at fifty coins, the

55
00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.150
best plan jumps to six and two:

56
00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:39.810
three hundred and eighty.

57
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.780
The shape didn’t move; only

58
00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.220
the profit line tilted.

59
00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.530
And profit isn’t the garden’s only goal.

60
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:52.900
It’s also for learning: maybe every

61
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.480
class should get a bed of each.

62
00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.430
Say so, and it becomes one more limit.

63
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.320
Agree the goal before you

64
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.440
trust the corner.

65
00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.540
In the app, drag the profit to aim

66
00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.640
for up, slowly, and watch where the

67
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.460
dashed line leaves the shape.

68
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.340
Then change a price or the hours,

69
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.480
and see the best corner move.

70
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.300
Your turn.

71
00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.330
If pupils could give twenty-four hours a

72
00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:24.010
week instead of eighteen, would the best

73
00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.590
plan stay at three and five?

74
00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.880
No: the hours line moves out, and the

75
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.940
best plan becomes eight bean beds,

76
00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:33.660
three hundred and twenty coins.

77
00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.970
Remember: each limit is a line, the

78
00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.030
feasible plans are the shaded shape,

79
00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:41.450
and the best plan is at a corner.

80
00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.930
Move a limit or a price, and check again.

81
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.740
Try it in class tomorrow: give each group

82
00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.320
graph paper and the garden’s three limits.

83
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.580
Pupils draw the lines, shade the

84
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.040
plans that fit, and work out the

85
00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.320
profit at each corner.

86
00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.790
Which corner wins, and

87
00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:01.690
would they plant it?
